# Fail Fast

When the system reaches a state it does not expect, stop and raise rather than continue.

## Raise on unexpected state

If a precondition is not met, an invariant is broken, or input is outside the contract, raise a
clear error at the point of detection. Do not continue with a guessed value or a partial result.

## Prefer strict variants

When an operation has both a "strict" form that raises and a "lenient" form that skips, choose the
strict form. Loud failure during development or deployment is far cheaper than silent drift
discovered days later.

For example, a table rename should use a strict helper that raises when state is inconsistent rather
than a wrapper that skips when the table is missing.

## Keep failures diagnosable

A raised error should say what was expected and what was found, so the failure points directly at the
cause. This complements `no-silent-fallback`: never swallow the condition you just detected.
